---
import { OFFER } from '../config/offer';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BreadcrumbsEnhanced from '../components/seo/BreadcrumbsEnhanced.astro';
import FeaturedAnswer from '../components/seo/FeaturedAnswer.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/seo/ComparisonTable.astro';
import FAQEnhanced from '../components/seo/FAQEnhanced.astro';

// This page deliberately does NOT claim DriveSchoolPro is accounting software.
// It records lesson income and issues invoices; it does not do bookkeeping, expenses,
// mileage or a Self Assessment return. Saying so plainly is the point of the page —
// ADIs searching this term are trying to work out which tool does which job.
const comparisonRows = [
  { label: 'Record lesson income per pupil', values: [true, 'Manual entry'], highlight: true },
  { label: 'Issue invoices to pupils', values: [true, true] },
  { label: 'Track outstanding balances', values: [true, true] },
  { label: 'Lesson packages with expiry dates', values: [true, false], highlight: true },
  { label: 'Revenue reports by period', values: [true, true] },
  { label: 'Categorise business expenses', values: [false, true] },
  { label: 'Mileage tracking', values: [false, true] },
  { label: 'Bank feed reconciliation', values: [false, true] },
  { label: 'Self Assessment / MTD submission', values: [false, true] },
  { label: 'Knows what a pupil, lesson or test is', values: [true, false], highlight: true },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Do driving instructors need an accountant?',
    answer:
      'No. Many self-employed ADIs keep their own records and file their own Self Assessment return. An accountant is worth paying for in your first tax year, when you move to Making Tax Digital (sole traders with lesson income over £30,000 start on 6 April 2027), when you buy or lease a car, if you go limited or take on other instructors, or as you approach the £90,000 VAT threshold. Accountancy fees are an allowable business expense.',
    links: [{ text: 'Making Tax Digital for driving instructors', url: '/blog/making-tax-digital-driving-instructors/' }],
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I ask an accountant for driving instructors?',
    answer:
      'Ask whether they work with other driving instructors and how they treat franchise fees, dual controls and car costs; whether they handle Making Tax Digital for Income Tax and which software you will need; whether the fee is fixed and includes the quarterly updates; and which records they want from you, how often and in what format.',
    links: [{ text: 'MTD record keeping for driving instructors', url: '/blog/mtd-record-keeping-driving-instructors/' }],
  },
  {
    question: 'Is DriveSchoolPro accounting software?',
    answer:
      'No, and we would rather say so up front. DriveSchoolPro records lesson income, issues invoices, tracks outstanding balances and reports revenue by period — the income side of your books, captured automatically as you teach. It can export your income as a CSV for your accountant (Settings, then Income Export). It does not categorise expenses, track mileage, reconcile a bank feed or file a Self Assessment return, and it is not Making Tax Digital software. For those you still want proper accounting software or an accountant. The two work well together: DriveSchoolPro is where the income data comes from, accounting software is where the tax return happens.',
    links: [{ text: 'What DriveSchoolPro does do', url: '/driving-school-management-software/' }],
  },
  {
    question: 'What accounting software is best for driving instructors?',
    answer:
      'For most self-employed ADIs, general small-business accounting software is the right answer — the market leaders all handle sole-trader Self Assessment and Making Tax Digital, and none of them need to understand driving lessons to do that job well. What they cannot do is tell you which pupil owes for which lesson. That is the gap instructor software fills. Run both: instructor software for the lesson-level detail, accounting software for the tax return.',
    links: [],
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the best accounting app for driving instructors in the UK?',
    answer:
      "There are two kinds. General accounting software handles the tax side: expenses, mileage, bank feeds, Self Assessment and Making Tax Digital submissions, and HMRC lists the products that can file MTD updates. Some driving instructor apps also add an accounts feature, according to their own sites: GoRoadie Pro lists photo-scanned expense receipts, mileage and a QuickBooks link, and says it is Making Tax Digital ready; MyDriveTime lists an income and expense report and a Making Tax Digital set-up with Xero or CSV options; Total Drive links to QuickBooks, Xero, FreeAgent, Sage and FBTC or CSV. DriveSchoolPro is not accounting software: it records every lesson, invoice and payment so the income figures are right, can export that income as a CSV, and you pair it with accounting software or an accountant for the return. Features were checked on the vendors' own sites on 19 November 2026.",
    links: [
      { text: 'GoRoadie vs DriveSchoolPro', url: '/compare/goroadie/' },
      { text: 'MyDriveTime vs DriveSchoolPro', url: '/compare/my-drive-time/' },
      { text: 'Total Drive vs DriveSchoolPro', url: '/compare/total-drive/' },
    ],
  },
  {
    question: 'How do driving instructors keep track of lesson income?',
    answer:
      'Badly, if it lives in a paper diary. The common failure is not fraud or carelessness — it is that lesson income arrives in small amounts, from many people, by several methods, often in cash, across a week where you are driving rather than filing. By the time you reconcile, you are reconstructing from memory. Recording the payment against the lesson at the moment it happens removes the reconstruction entirely, and it is the single biggest bookkeeping improvement most ADIs can make.',
    links: [{ text: 'Switching from a paper diary', url: '/blog/switch-paper-diary-to-driving-school-software/' }],
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I claim my driving school software as a business expense?',
    answer:
      'Software used wholly and exclusively for your instructing business is generally an allowable business expense for a self-employed ADI, alongside things like your ADI registration, insurance and vehicle costs. The rules on apportionment matter if something is used partly personally, so check your own circumstances with an accountant rather than relying on a software vendor for tax advice. We are not qualified to give it and will not pretend otherwise.',
    links: [],
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need to worry about Making Tax Digital as a driving instructor?',
    answer:
      'Probably, if you teach full-time. GOV.UK (checked 19 November 2026) says self-employed people must use Making Tax Digital for Income Tax from 6 April 2026 if their qualifying income for 2024 to 2025 was over £50,000, from 6 April 2027 if it was over £30,000 for 2025 to 2026, and from 6 April 2028 if it was over £20,000 for 2026 to 2027. Qualifying income is turnover before expenses, so at about £35 a lesson anyone teaching more than roughly 19 paid hours a week is over £30,000. Our guide walks through the thresholds, quarterly deadlines and a checklist.',
    links: [{ text: 'Making Tax Digital for driving instructors', url: '/blog/making-tax-digital-driving-instructors/' }],
  },
  {
    question: 'Does DriveSchoolPro submit Making Tax Digital updates to HMRC?',
    answer:
      'No. DriveSchoolPro is not Making Tax Digital software and does not send anything to HMRC. What it can do is export your income as a CSV file (Settings, then Income Export): one row per payment or refund, with monthly totals, for the tax year or any dates you choose. Your accountant or your MTD-compatible software can use that for the income side. Expenses and mileage are not in it.',
    links: [{ text: 'MTD record keeping for driving instructors', url: '/blog/mtd-record-keeping-driving-instructors/' }],
  },
  {
    question: 'Does DriveSchoolPro handle cash payments?',
    answer:
      'Yes. You can record payments taken by cash, bank transfer or cheque against the lesson and pupil they belong to, which is what makes the income side reconcile later. You can also take card payments through Stripe pay-links on invoices — we charge 3% on card payments you collect, and Stripe\'s standard processing fee applies on top. Cash is still common in driving instruction, and software that assumes every payment is a card transaction quietly fails the people who need the record most.',
    links: [{ text: 'Get started free', url: OFFER.ctaPrimary.href }],
  },
];

const schemaMarkup = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Best Accounting App for Driving Instructors in the UK',
  description:
    'What driving instructor accounting software does, what instructor management software does, and why most self-employed ADIs need both.',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DriveSchoolPro' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DriveSchoolPro' },
  datePublished: '2026-08-19',
  dateModified: '2026-11-19',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Best Accounting App for Driving Instructors UK (2026)"
  description="Which accounting app suits a UK driving instructor? Compare GoRoadie Pro, MyDriveTime, Total Drive and DriveSchoolPro on expenses, mileage and MTD."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schemaMarkup)} />

  <BreadcrumbsEnhanced
    site="driveschool"
    items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Driving Instructor Accounting Software', href: '/driving-instructor-accounting-software/' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-gradient-to-br from-brand-navy to-gray-900 text-white py-16">
    <div class="container-custom relative">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <span class="inline-block px-4 py-1 bg-brand-red/20 text-brand-red-light rounded-full text-sm font-medium mb-4">Finance &amp; Admin</span>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">Best Accounting App for Driving Instructors in the UK</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-8">
          Most ADIs searching this are trying to solve two different problems with one tool. Here is which job belongs to accounting software, which instructor apps include accounts features (checked 19 November 2026), and where DriveSchoolPro fits.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
          <a href={OFFER.ctaPrimary.href} class="btn-primary text-lg px-8 py-4">Try DriveSchoolPro Free</a>
          <a href="#compare-apps" class="btn-secondary text-lg px-8 py-4">Compare the apps</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FeaturedAnswer question="What is the best accounting app for driving instructors in the UK?">
    It depends which job you mean. For the tax return itself (expenses, mileage, bank feed, Self Assessment and Making Tax Digital updates), the answer is general accounting software that appears on HMRC's list of compatible software, or an accountant who uses it. If you would rather keep expenses inside your instructor app, three UK apps list accounts features on their own sites: GoRoadie Pro (expense and receipt tracking, mileage, QuickBooks link), MyDriveTime (income and expense report, receipts, a Making Tax Digital set-up with Xero or CSV) and Total Drive (links to QuickBooks, Xero, FreeAgent, Sage and FBTC, or CSV). DriveSchoolPro is not accounting software: it records every lesson, invoice and payment and can export that income as a CSV for your accountant, but it does not track expenses or mileage or send anything to HMRC.
  </FeaturedAnswer>

  <section class="section bg-white" id="two-jobs">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">The two jobs people mean by "accounting"</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        When an instructor searches for accounting software, they usually want one of two quite different things, and buying the wrong one is why so many ADIs end up with a subscription they never open.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        <strong>Job one is the tax return.</strong> Expenses categorised, mileage logged, bank transactions reconciled, and a set of numbers that survives contact with HMRC. This is genuine bookkeeping, it is well served by mainstream accounting software, and nothing about it is specific to driving instruction. A tool built for plumbers does it just as well.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        <strong>Job two is knowing who owes you money.</strong> Which pupil has hours left on a package, who paid cash on Tuesday, whose block booking expires this month, and what you actually earned last week versus what you invoiced. This is not bookkeeping — it is operational, it happens daily, and general accounting software is terrible at it because it has no concept of a pupil or a lesson.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-8">
        The failure mode is trying to run job two inside accounting software. You end up creating a "customer" per pupil and an invoice per lesson by hand, which is more admin than the paper diary you were escaping.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">Why lesson income is unusually hard to track</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        Driving instruction has a genuinely awkward income profile. Payments are small, frequent, and come from many different people. They arrive by bank transfer, in cash, and sometimes in advance as a block booking that is consumed over months. And crucially, you are driving for most of the working day, so nothing gets recorded at the moment it happens.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        Block bookings are the specific trap. A pupil pays for ten hours up front. That money arrives in one month and is earned across the next three. If your only record is a bank statement, your monthly income looks lumpy and unrelated to the work you did, and you cannot answer the one question that matters operationally: how many hours does this pupil have left?
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-8">
        Recording the payment against the pupil and the lesson at the point it happens solves both problems at once. Your income record reflects work delivered, and the remaining balance on a package is simply always correct.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">What DriveSchoolPro does — and what it does not</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        We would rather be clear than sell you something on a misunderstanding. DriveSchoolPro is not accounting software and does not try to be.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        <strong>It does:</strong> record payments against pupils and lessons by cash, bank transfer or cheque; create and send invoices with line items and track them through their lifecycle; manage lesson packages with configurable hours, pricing and expiry; show outstanding balances; and report revenue, lessons delivered, cancellations and no-shows by period; take card payments through Stripe pay-links on invoices; and export your income as a CSV for your accountant (Settings, then Income Export).
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-8">
        <strong>It does not:</strong> categorise business expenses, track mileage, connect to a bank feed, produce a profit-and-loss statement for HMRC, link directly to accounting software, or file anything with HMRC. It is not Making Tax Digital software. For all of that you want accounting software or an accountant — and the income figures from DriveSchoolPro are what you hand them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white" id="compare-apps">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-5xl">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-4">Instructor apps with accounts features: a fair comparison</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        Three UK instructor apps list expense or accounting features alongside the diary. DriveSchoolPro does not, and the table says so. Everything in the competitor rows comes from each vendor's own website, checked on <strong>19 November 2026</strong>; &ldquo;Not listed&rdquo; means we could not find it on the pages we checked, not that it is missing. Pricing changes, so check each vendor's own pricing page.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200 bg-white">
          <caption class="sr-only">Accounts features in UK driving instructor apps, from vendors' own sites, checked 19 November 2026</caption>
          <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-brand-navy">
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">App</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Expenses and receipts</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Mileage</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Getting figures to an accountant or HMRC software</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Making Tax Digital on its own site</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-gray-700 align-top">
            <tr class="border-t border-gray-200">
              <th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">GoRoadie Pro</th>
              <td class="p-3">Listed: expense tracking and photo-scanned receipts</td>
              <td class="p-3">Listed (also on its Web Dashboard)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Spreadsheet export you choose by dates and pupils, and a QuickBooks integration</td>
              <td class="p-3">Says &ldquo;You&rsquo;re Making Tax Digital ready&rdquo;</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-gray-200">
              <th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">MyDriveTime</th>
              <td class="p-3">Listed: income and expense report, and receipts attached to outgoing payments</td>
              <td class="p-3">Not listed on the pages we checked</td>
              <td class="p-3">Four routes on its Making Tax Digital set-up: managed with FBTC, hybrid or connect with Xero, or CSV export. Third-party fees are paid to those providers</td>
              <td class="p-3">Has a Making Tax Digital set-up in Settings</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-gray-200">
              <th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Total Drive</th>
              <td class="p-3">Listed: income and expenses, mapped to categories</td>
              <td class="p-3">Listed: mileage log</td>
              <td class="p-3">Connects to QuickBooks, Xero, FreeAgent, Sage and FBTC, or CSV export of income and expenses; you choose when to sync</td>
              <td class="p-3">Has a Making Tax Digital section with accounting integrations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-gray-200">
              <th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">DriveSchoolPro</th>
              <td class="p-3">No</td>
              <td class="p-3">No</td>
              <td class="p-3">Income CSV: one row per payment or refund plus monthly totals, for a tax year or dates you choose</td>
              <td class="p-3">Not MTD software; does not submit to HMRC</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-6">
        Sources:
        <a href="https://www.goroadie.com/pro" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">GoRoadie Pro</a>,
        <a href="https://www.goroadie.com/press/2026-09-goroadie-preps-autumn-update" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">GoRoadie autumn 2026 update</a>,
        <a href="https://www.mydrivetime.co.uk/" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">MyDriveTime</a>,
        <a href="https://www.mydrivetime.co.uk/blog/mydrivetime-solves-making-tax-digital-for-you/" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">MyDriveTime on Making Tax Digital</a>,
        <a href="https://totaldrive.co.uk/making-tax-digital/accounting-integrations-for-driving-instructors/" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">Total Drive accounting integrations</a>.
        Whether each app files updates with HMRC itself, or leaves that to the connected software, is not something we have verified: check HMRC&rsquo;s list before you rely on it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-brand-navy mb-3">How to choose</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li><strong>You want expenses, receipts and mileage in the same app as your diary:</strong> look at GoRoadie Pro, MyDriveTime or Total Drive first. DriveSchoolPro cannot do this today.</li>
        <li><strong>You already have an accountant or accounting software:</strong> any app that gets clean income figures to them will do. DriveSchoolPro&rsquo;s income CSV is built for that handover.</li>
        <li><strong>You want the cleanest record of who paid for which lesson:</strong> that is what DriveSchoolPro is built around, with payments recorded against lessons, packages and invoices.</li>
        <li><strong>You need to send Making Tax Digital updates:</strong> pick software from <a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/find-software-thats-compatible-with-making-tax-digital-for-income-tax" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">HMRC&rsquo;s list of compatible software</a>, or ask your accountant. Our <a href="/blog/choosing-mtd-software-driving-instructors/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">guide to choosing MTD software</a> has the questions to ask.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-700">
        For the side-by-side detail on each app, see
        <a href="/compare/goroadie/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">GoRoadie Pro vs DriveSchoolPro</a>,
        <a href="/compare/my-drive-time/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">MyDriveTime vs DriveSchoolPro</a> and
        <a href="/compare/total-drive/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">Total Drive vs DriveSchoolPro</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-gray-50">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <ComparisonTable
        title="Instructor software vs accounting software"
        description="Which tool does which job. The honest answer for most self-employed ADIs is that you want both, doing different things."
        headers={['Capability', 'DriveSchoolPro', 'General accounting software']}
        rows={comparisonRows}
      />
      <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-4">
        Nothing here is tax advice. Thresholds, allowable expenses and Making Tax Digital timing change — check GOV.UK or an accountant for your own position.
        To estimate what you might owe, try the <a href="/tools/adi-income-tax-calculator/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">ADI income and tax estimator</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">A workable setup for a solo ADI</h2>
      <ol class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 mb-8">
        <li><strong>1. Record every payment against the lesson when it happens.</strong> In the car, on your phone, before you drive off. This is the whole discipline; everything else follows from it.</li>
        <li><strong>2. Invoice from the same place.</strong> If invoices are generated from lesson records, they cannot disagree with what you taught.</li>
        <li><strong>3. Keep expenses somewhere separate and boring.</strong> Fuel, maintenance, insurance, ADI registration, CPD, and your software subscriptions. Accounting software or a spreadsheet — the tool matters far less than doing it weekly.</li>
        <li><strong>4. Reconcile monthly, not annually.</strong> Compare the revenue report to what actually hit your bank. A month of discrepancies is solvable; a year of them is a weekend you will never get back.</li>
        <li><strong>5. Hand your accountant the income CSV or revenue report.</strong> Rather than a diary and a carrier bag of receipts.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="text-gray-700">
        If you are still running the diary side on paper, the operational change is bigger than the bookkeeping one — see
        <a href="/blog/switch-paper-diary-to-driving-school-software/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">switching from a paper diary</a>, or the
        <a href="/driving-school-management-software/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">management software overview</a> for what else moves at the same time. If you are comparing platforms on price,
        <a href="/blog/best-driving-school-software/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">the software roundup</a> compares seven of them feature by feature.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mt-4">
        Getting ready for Making Tax Digital? See
        <a href="/blog/mtd-record-keeping-driving-instructors/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">MTD record keeping for driving instructors</a>,
        <a href="/blog/choosing-mtd-software-driving-instructors/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">choosing MTD software</a> and the
        <a href="/blog/mtd-timeline-2027-2028-driving-instructors/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">MTD timeline for 2027 and 2028</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-gray-50" id="accountant">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">Do driving instructors need an accountant?</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
        No law says you must use one. Plenty of self-employed ADIs keep their own records and file their own Self Assessment
        return. An accountant earns their fee when your tax affairs stop being simple, and the fee itself is an allowable business
        expense.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-brand-navy mb-3">When an accountant for driving instructors is worth paying for</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Your first tax year</strong>, to set up records and expense categories you can keep using.</li>
        <li><strong>Switching to Making Tax Digital.</strong> Sole traders with lesson income over £30,000 a year start on 6 April 2027 and send quarterly updates. Many accountants file those updates for you. See <a href="/blog/making-tax-digital-driving-instructors/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">Making Tax Digital for driving instructors</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Buying, financing or leasing a car</strong>, and choosing between the flat mileage rate and actual car costs. The choice affects your tax for as long as you keep the car.</li>
        <li><strong>Going limited or taking on other instructors</strong>, where company accounts, payroll or associate agreements come in.</li>
        <li><strong>Getting close to the VAT registration threshold</strong> (£90,000 of taxable turnover in a rolling 12 months).</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-brand-navy mb-3">What to ask before you hire one</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li>Do you work with other driving instructors, and how do you treat franchise fees, dual controls and car costs?</li>
        <li>Do you handle Making Tax Digital for Income Tax, and which software will I need to use?</li>
        <li>Is your fee fixed for the year, and does it include the quarterly updates?</li>
        <li>What records do you want from me, how often, and in what format?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-700">
        Whoever does your books, the income figures start with your lessons. DriveSchoolPro records every lesson, invoice and payment
        and reports revenue by period, so you can give your accountant a report rather than a diary. It does not track expenses or file
        anything with HMRC. For the records your accountant will expect, see
        <a href="/blog/mtd-record-keeping-driving-instructors/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">MTD record keeping for driving instructors</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-brand-navy text-white text-center">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-3">Get the Income Side Right — Free Until 31 March 2027</h2>
      <p class="text-white/80 text-lg mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">Record payments against lessons, invoice from the same records, and hand your accountant a report instead of a diary.</p>
      <a href={OFFER.ctaPrimary.href} class="inline-block bg-brand-red text-white px-10 py-4 rounded-xl font-semibold text-lg hover:bg-red-700 transition-colors">
        {OFFER.ctaPrimary.label} &rarr;
      </a>
      <p class="text-white/50 text-sm mt-3">{OFFER.short} &middot; No credit card required &middot; Cancel anytime</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQEnhanced
    faqs={faqItems}
    title="Driving Instructor Accounting — Common Questions"
    site="driveschool"
    pageUrl="https://driveschoolpro.com/driving-instructor-accounting-software/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
